3,Ufim language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/ufim_language,"Ufim is a Trans-New Guinea language spoken by approximately 500 people in villages along the upper Umi and Ufim rivers in Morobe Province, Papua New Guinea. [1]",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
4,"Ufim Language (UFI) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/ufi/,"Ufim is a stable indigenous language of Papua New Guinea. It belongs to the Trans-New Guinea language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is not known to be taught in schools.",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
5,TransNewGuinea.org,https://www.transnewguinea.org/,"The Trans-New Guinea language family currently occupies most of the interior of New Guinea. This family is possibly the third largest in the world with 400 languages and is tentatively thought to have originated with root-crop agriculture around 10,000 years ago.",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
6,Trans–New Guinea languages — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Trans–New_Guinea_languages,"The Trans–New Guinea (TNG) languages are primarily distributed across the central cordillera of New Guinea, spanning both Papua New Guinea and the Indonesian provinces of Papua and West Papua, with extensions into the southern lowlands and northern coastal fringes.",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
7,database of the languages of New Guinea,https://transnewguinea.org/language/ufim,"Glottocode: ufim1240 ISO 639: ufi. Sources: McElhanon and Voorhoeve (1970). Classification: Trans-New Guinea, Finisterre-Huon, Finisterre, Gusap-Mot.",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
8,Ufim,https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Ufim.htm,"Language name and location: Ufim, Papua New Guinea [Refer to Ethnologue].Other comments: Ufim is spoken by approximately 1,300 speakers in five villages at the Upper Umi and Ufim River areas north of Kaiapit, Morobe Province, Papua New Guinea.",Ufim,Trans-New Guinea
